1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,800
 In this lecture, we are going to look at the second option for the Gen AI API layer,

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,720
 which is AWS AppSync.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,920
 So we need to know about this service at a high level.

4
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:12,480
 So let's go ahead and understand what this service does.

5
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:20,400
 So AWS AppSync is ideal for Gen AI chat and live dashboard applications.

6
00:00:20,400 --> 00:00:24,160
 So in the previous lecture, we have learned about web socket API,

7
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:29,920
 which maintain a persistent connection and are great for Gen AI chat and live dashboard.

8
00:00:30,160 --> 00:00:35,920
 So the use case scenarios for AWS AppSync are very similar to the web socket API.

9
00:00:35,920 --> 00:00:39,120
 And the only key difference is that in web socket API,

10
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:41,120
 you have to manage the routes.

11
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:43,760
 Now what is a primary function?

12
00:00:43,760 --> 00:00:48,400
 So AWS AppSync is a fully managed service for building GraphQL.

13
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:49,600
 So what is GraphQL?

14
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:52,400
 So GraphQL was developed by Facebook.

15
00:00:52,400 --> 00:00:59,120
 It's a query language for APIs that allow clients to request exactly the data

16
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:00,320
 they require.

17
00:01:00,320 --> 00:01:03,280
 So if we compare it to the REST API,

18
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:08,480
 if we are trying to retrieve the data using the REST API versus the GraphQL,

19
00:01:08,480 --> 00:01:15,440
 it's going to give exact response based on the client query compared to the REST API,

20
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:17,760
 which may under fetch or over fetch.

21
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:23,200
 So with the GraphQL, you can retrieve data from multiple related data sources

22
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,440
 in one API call instead of making separate requests for each resource.

23
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,440
 Now let's say we have multiple data sources and user has posed a question

24
00:01:31,440 --> 00:01:34,880
 where you need to fetch data from these different data sources.

25
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,160
 So if you use REST API,

26
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:41,200
 maybe you will have to make four API calls for each of the different data sources.

27
00:01:41,200 --> 00:01:44,320
 Now so for a similar scenario, if you use GraphQL,

28
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:50,080
 then you will have to just make one request and GraphQL should be able to retrieve the data

29
00:01:50,080 --> 00:01:54,640
 from these different data sources through just one API call.

30
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,160
 Okay, what's the API type protocol?

31
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:03,040
 So GraphQL over HTTP, HTTPS, and web sockets.

32
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:09,120
 Okay, now what are the additional core features for AWS AppSync?

33
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:11,360
 It provides a real-time subscription.

34
00:02:11,360 --> 00:02:15,680
 So what that means is once the client has subscribed,

35
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:20,880
 then he's going to get auto push updates from the server side.

36
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:23,120
 So that's useful for live dashboard.

37
00:02:23,120 --> 00:02:26,640
 So once you have subscribed to live dashboards,

38
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:29,360
 as there are changes, those changes are going to be

39
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:32,720
 pushed and visible to the client or the user.

40
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:34,560
 Then it also provides offline support.

41
00:02:34,560 --> 00:02:37,840
 This is especially good for mobile or web.

42
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:40,960
 So if you don't have internet, then you can get offline support.

43
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:45,600
 So it integrates with Lambda for orchestration and bedrock for inference.

44
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,320
 Some of the key use cases we already discussed,

45
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:51,840
 chatbots dashboards requiring live updates.

46
00:02:51,840 --> 00:02:54,080
 Now let's try and understand this through a use case.

47
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:58,480
 So let's say we have an end user who is accessing a JNA application

48
00:02:58,480 --> 00:03:02,240
 using web or mobile device and he asks a question.

49
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:05,440
 And this is sometimes called as mutation,

50
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:09,920
 which you can think of like a state change or just simply a trigger.

51
00:03:09,920 --> 00:03:15,920
 Now this AWS AppSync sends that request or prompt to AWS Lambda.

52
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,880
 And it's an asynchronous invocation.

53
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:20,160
 It's not synchronous.

54
00:03:20,160 --> 00:03:24,320
 So it does not wait for response from Lambda.

55
00:03:24,320 --> 00:03:28,320
 So once this request goes to AWS Lambda,

56
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:31,520
 then it is going to be sent to Amazon bedrock.

57
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:36,800
 And Amazon bedrock is going to stream the response in chunks.

58
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:40,560
 So as the response gets generated and is sent,

59
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:46,080
 this AWS Lambda function is going to send that answer to the AWS AppSync,

60
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:50,880
 which is going to send that response back to all the clients

61
00:03:50,880 --> 00:03:53,520
 that have subscribed to this event.

62
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:58,960
 So there could be multiple clients or users who have subscribed to this live dashboard

63
00:03:58,960 --> 00:04:01,280
 as the answer is getting generated.

64
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,720
 All the subscribers who have subscribed to this event

65
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:06,560
 are going to get the updates.

66
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:09,680
 Okay, so that's pretty much it from this lecture.

67
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:10,640
 I'll see in the next lecture.

68
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:11,120
 Thank you.

